The stronger paper-native option is viable: Lemma 4 (Section 2.2) proves that every chordal, induced-\(2P_3\)-free graph has a clique whose deletion is a cluster graph, and Section 3 gives the clique-tree construction an \(O(n^3)\) bound. I’m building that exact witness problem—not a generic planted CVD instance—and generating promised graphs from nested clique blocks so the hitting clique is known before the graph is assembled. This makes it honestly Track B: the polynomial clique-tree method will be reported as successful, not smuggled into the failing attack panel.
codex
I’m implementing the family as a succinct but exact \(G^+\): original vertices are bit-vectors, generator masks define Cayley edges, and every original vertex gets its paper-prescribed pendant. A submitted parity functional denotes the entire deletion set, so the witness stays writable even though the graph is enormous. Generation samples the functional first; verification checks edge alternation and uses a fixed generator’s perfect matching to certify optimality.
