+NOTES = r"""
+Definition and theorem source.  The Introduction defines a cluster graph as a
+disjoint union of cliques and a hitting set X by the condition that G-X is a
+cluster graph; equivalently X hits every induced P3.  Section 2.2 then defines a
+hitting clique and Lemma 4 proves that every chordal, induced-2P3-free graph
+contains one.  The proof constructs a clique tree, orients each tree edge
+towards its non-cluster side, and takes a sink clique.  The generated task asks
+for exactly this native graph object.
+
+Step-0 algorithm audit.  This cannot be Track A.  Lemma 4 explicitly says the
+hitting clique is polynomial-time constructible, and the proof of Lemma 8 in
+Section 3 bounds the relevant clique-tree construction by O(N^3).  The paper
+also records 1.811^k n^{O(1)} FPT and O(1.488^n) exact algorithms for general
+Cluster Vertex Deletion, as well as its O(N^4) 2-approximation.  Here the
+reference clique-tree/PEO algorithm is therefore expected to solve every
+instance and is reported separately.  Track B measures the gap between that
+mechanical scan and recognizing the deliberately nested clique blocks.
+
+Generation.  A clique K is sampled first.  The remaining vertices are split
+into two cliques C0,C1 with no edges between them.  Each outside vertex receives
+a threshold t in {1,...,|K|}; after ordering K as k1,...,kk, it is adjacent to
+ki exactly when i>t.  Thus every K-to-Cj neighborhood is nested.  K is a known
+hitting clique because deleting it leaves C0 disjoint from C1.  The graph is
+chordal: an induced cycle cannot use both outside cliques (two K vertices would
+give a chord), and within K union Cj nested cross-neighborhoods forbid an
+induced C4.  Every induced P3 meets K, so two disjoint induced P3s have an edge
+between their K vertices; hence the graph is induced-2P3-free.  A final uniform
+vertex relabelling removes positional information.  Seed-dependent block sizes
+and threshold multiplicities give genuinely non-isomorphic instances.
+
+Attack defenses.  The stored clique spans the full degree range, while the two
+outside cliques overlap its middle degrees; taking degree outliers fails.  The
+same overlap defeats largest-first clique greed.  Random restarts sample the
+statement-aware k-subset language.  The in-context degree-band ansatz chooses
+the k vertices nearest the median degree and also fails.  Unlike these attacks,
+the reference algorithm enumerates maximal cliques from a perfect-elimination
+ordering and tests the cluster remainder, so it succeeds as Track B requires.
+
+Canonicalization.  Vertex labels are the only relabelling symmetry exposed by
+the statement.  canonical_key performs canonical 1-dimensional Weisfeiler--
+Leman refinement from degrees and hashes the resulting colour quotient (class
+sizes and all between-class edge counts).  It is invariant under every vertex
+permutation.  This is an invariant, not a complete graph canonizer, so in
+principle non-isomorphic graphs can collide; the 20-seed distinctness test is
+reported and passes for the shipping distribution.
+""".strip()
