+# arXiv:2007.08057 — verified Cluster Vertex Deletion generator
+
+Status: **locally verified, but not yet oracle-hardened**. The required OpenRouter
+run was attempted for all three arms and every request returned HTTP 403, “Key
+limit exceeded (total limit).” The script-owned error transcripts are preserved;
+there is no fabricated hardness verdict.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — no-tool compression |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate form | exact symbolic |
+| Intuition | invariant: the global XOR of the Cayley generators is the hidden parity functional |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed, Proposition 18 |
+
+This is representational coverage through the paper's own reduction, not native
+coverage of unrestricted explicit Cluster Vertex Deletion graphs. The source
+object is a succinct Cayley graph over GF(2); Proposition 18 turns it into the
+paper's pendant-vertex Cluster Vertex Deletion graph.
+
+## Problem and trust model
+
+The source paper is Aprile, Drescher, Fiorini, and Huynh,
+[“A Tight Approximation Algorithm for the Cluster Vertex Deletion Problem”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.08057).
+The Introduction defines a hitting set as vertices whose deletion leaves a
+disjoint union of cliques. Proposition 18 attaches one pendant vertex to every
+vertex of a graph `G` and proves that subsets of the original vertices are
+Cluster-VD hitting sets exactly when they are vertex covers of `G`.
+
+An instance gives generator masks `s` for a Cayley graph on GF(2)^d. The answer
+`[w,b]` symbolically denotes all original vertices `x` satisfying
+`<w,x> = b`. The checker verifies, with exact integer bit operations, that
+`<w,s> = 1` for every generator. This alternation covers every Cayley edge. Any
+one nonzero generator supplies a perfect matching of size `2^(d-1)`, so the
+represented cover—and hence the corresponding Cluster-VD hitting set—is
+minimum. The generator samples `w` first, samples every displayed mask from the
+same affine hyperplane, forces their XOR to be `w`, checks full rank, and only
+then emits the instance. It never solves its own output.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Track A would be false. The paper gives a polynomial 2-approximation (Theorem 1,
+with `O(N^4)` analysis in Section 3), and Section 1.3 records exact
+`1.811^k N^O(1)` and `O(1.488^N)` algorithms. This generated distribution has an
+even stronger exact method: Gaussian elimination on the `m` by `d` system
+`<w,s>=1`, taking `O(m d^2)` scalar-bit work. At the provisional shipping preset
+`d=72,m=216`, the reference implementation solved 8/8 instances and averaged
+474,459 scalar-bit operations (0.0030 seconds in the final report).
+
+The compressed route is qualitatively different: XOR all 216 displayed masks.
+By construction their XOR is `w`, so either side bit is valid. That is 215
+word-XORs—within the 300-operation cap, but not a plausible dense elimination to
+perform unaided in a chat response. The intended test is noticing the global
+invariant. Four cheaper approaches (row outliers, greedy pivots without
+elimination, 256 random restarts, and obvious all-ones/unit/partial-XOR guesses)
+all failed on 8/8 seeds.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=4, m=4, seed=3)`, the complete displayed data are:
+
+```text
+d = 4
+generator masks:
+  000: 2
+  001: 9
+  002: 5
+  003: d
+minimum deletion size: 8
+answer format: ["w", b], where w is one lowercase hex digit and b is 0 or 1
+```
+
+XOR gives `2 XOR 9 XOR 5 XOR d = 3`, so one answer is:
+
+```python
+>>> inst["answer"]
+["3", 0]
+>>> verify(inst, ["3", 0])
+(True, "ok")
+>>> verify(inst, ["3", 2])
+(False, "b must be the integer 0 or 1")
+```
+
+A person can solve this demo on paper: it needs three one-digit XORs and four
+parity checks.
+
+## Presets
+
+| Preset | d (`n`) | generators `m` | answer atoms | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 4 | 2 | hand-solvable illustration |
+| easy | 72 | 216 | 2 | provisional `SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` |
+| medium | 80 | 240 | 2 | local gates pass |
+| hard | 96 | 288 | 2 | local gates pass |
+
+No preset has an oracle verdict because the provider rejected all requests
+before a model answered. `easy` remains provisional; it must not be treated as
+a corpus-ready shipping level until `harden.py` records `hardened`.
+
+## Gate measurements
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 16/16 planted witnesses; JSON-native |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected for five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged prose/fence round-trip recovered and verified |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; exact density `1/(2^72-1)` |
+| G5 | pass | demo has exactly 2 answers; shipping reference cost 474,459 scalar-bit ops |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks 0/8; Gaussian reference 8/8; XOR route 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubled dimension 72→144, fixed two-atom answer, planted witness verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 140/140 relabellings invariant and carried witnesses valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 24 chars, about 6 tokens, 2 atoms, 215 intended operations |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The bare script-owned transcript contains these failed redraws:
+
+| Preset | Seed | Model | Result |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| easy | 1854791118 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | HTTP 403 quota error |
+| easy | 77766518 | Claude Sonnet 5 | HTTP 403 quota error |
+| easy | 900930090 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | HTTP 403 quota error |
+| easy | 449151096 | Claude Sonnet 5 | HTTP 403 quota error |
+
+| Arm | Solved / valid attempts | Provider-error redraws | Interpretation |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | 4 | unmeasured |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | unmeasured |
+| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | unmeasured |
+
+`hinted − placebo` is therefore undefined; the module records `0.0` only as a
+machine-readable placeholder for two empty arms. Nothing can be concluded about
+whether the XOR hint helps until the OpenRouter quota is restored.
+
+## Use
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```python
+import importlib.util
+import json
+
+path = "results/2007.08057/gen_2007_08057.py"
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("gen", path)
+gen = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(gen)
+
+params = gen.DIFFICULTY[gen.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = gen.make_instance(seed=123, **params)
+question = gen.render(inst)
+wire = "<answer>" + json.dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>"
+answer = gen.parse_answer(wire)
+ok, reason = gen.verify(inst, answer)
+```
+
+After a successful oracle-hardening rerun, emit with:
+
+```bash
+scripts/emit.sh 2007.08057 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The construction becomes easy as soon as the solver notices and accurately
+executes the all-generator XOR; that is intentional Track B behavior. The G4
+density is under a uniform prior over nonzero parity functionals and a side bit;
+it says nothing about a solver prior biased toward XOR combinations. Gaussian
+elimination was run and succeeds, as expected. No SAT/SMT or generic explicit-
+graph Cluster-VD implementation was run—the graph has `2^73` vertices at the
+shipping preset and is intentionally succinct. The checker validates the finite
+symbolic certificate rather than expanding that graph. `canonical_key` uses a
+four-circuit incidence invariant, not a complete matroid-isomorphism canonizer,
+so rare non-isomorphic collisions remain possible despite the 20/20 test. The
+largest caveat is decisive: the four-vendor hardening and all G9 diagnostics are
+still unmeasured because the configured OpenRouter key has exhausted its total
+limit.
