index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..36568edfe72635bc04c337efb3f29b904bb7cf25
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,187 @@
+# Cluster Vertex Deletion generator for arXiv:2007.08057
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate form | exact symbolic |
+| Intended intuition | invariant |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed, Proposition 18 |
+
+This module turns Aprile, Drescher, Fiorini, and Huynh’s [*A Tight Approximation
+Algorithm for the Cluster Vertex Deletion Problem*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.08057)
+into exact minimum-deletion problems. The solver receives a succinct Cayley graph
+on `GF(2)^d`, extended by one pendant vertex per original vertex, and returns a
+two-field symbolic description `[w,b]` of a parity-hyperplane deletion set. The
+checker uses only XOR, parity, and integer comparisons.
+
+The family is trustworthy by construction. Every generator mask `s` satisfies
+`<w,s>=1`, so every Cayley edge crosses the two parity classes and either class is
+a vertex cover. Any nonzero generator also pairs every `x` with `x XOR s`, giving
+a perfect matching of size `2^(d-1)` and proving that the cover is minimum.
+Proposition 18 proves that this cover is exactly a Cluster-VD hitting set after
+the pendant extension. The verifier executes the edge-alternation test and does
+not read `inst["answer"]`.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Section 1 defines Cluster-VD as minimum-cost deletion to a disjoint union of
+cliques. Theorem 1 gives a polynomial-time 2-approximation, Section 3 bounds its
+implementation by `O(N^4)`, and Section 1.3 records exact `1.811^k poly(N)` and
+`O(1.488^N)` algorithms. Those results make a Track A claim inappropriate for
+this specially structured distribution.
+
+For the succinct instances here, the direct exact algorithm solves the `m` dense
+equations `<w,s>=1` by GF(2) Gaussian elimination in `O(m d^2)`. At provisional
+shipping parameters `d=72,m=216`, it solved 8/8 instances in 0.00818 seconds on
+average with 474,459 counted scalar-bit operations. The compressed route notices
+that the XOR of all masks is `w`, taking 215 word-XORs. This is within the no-tool
+cap but far beyond casual guessing; it is also the principal caveat of the family.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The `demo` preset with seed 0 renders in full as follows:
+
+```text
+Minimum Cluster Vertex Deletion in a succinct graph
+
+All arithmetic on bit-vectors below is over GF(2). A d-bit vector is written as
+exactly 1 lowercase hexadecimal digits, including leading zeroes. XOR is
+bitwise exclusive-or. For vectors a and z, <a,z> is the parity (0 for even, 1
+for odd) of the number of 1-bits in a AND z.
+
+A cluster graph is an undirected simple graph whose connected components are
+complete graphs. A cluster vertex-deletion hitting set is a set of vertices
+whose deletion leaves a cluster graph.
+
+Here d=4. Define an undirected simple graph H as follows. For every d-bit
+vector x there are two vertices O_x (an original vertex) and P_x (its pendant).
+There is an edge O_x--P_x for every x. There is also an edge O_x--O_(x XOR s)
+for every x and every generator mask s in the list below. These are all edges;
+repeated descriptions of the same undirected edge count only once.
+
+Generator masks (4 total, indices are only labels):
+  000: c
+  001: 3
+  002: 4
+  003: d
+
+Your answer must be a pair [w,b]. Here w is a nonzero d-bit vector in exactly
+1 lowercase hexadecimal digits, and b is the integer 0 or 1. The pair
+symbolically denotes the deletion set
+
+    X(w,b) = { O_x : <w,x> = b }.
+
+No pendant P_x is deleted. Find any [w,b] for which X(w,b) is a minimum-cardinality
+cluster vertex-deletion hitting set of H. Both b choices are allowed if valid.
+The minimum deletion size is promised to be 8; you do
+not write out those vertices. Order in the two-element pair matters.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as a JSON pair
+["w",b], with the hexadecimal mask quoted and b unquoted.
+Example of format only: <answer>["1",0]</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>["6",0]</answer>`; `verify(inst, ["6",0])` returns
+`(True, "ok")`. The corrupted pair `["6","6"]` returns
+`(False, "b must be the integer 0 or 1")`. A person can solve this demo by
+XORing `c XOR 3 XOR 4 XOR d = 6`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | d | masks m | graph vertices | certificate candidates | compact operations | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 4 | 32 | 30 | 3 | hand-scale; harness skips it |
+| easy | 72 | 216 | `2^73` | `2(2^72-1)` | 215 | provisional shipping preset |
+| medium | 80 | 240 | `2^81` | `2(2^80-1)` | 239 | locally verified |
+| hard | 96 | 288 | `2^97` | `2(2^96-1)` | 287 | locally verified |
+
+No preset was rejected by a local gate. “Provisional” matters: the required
+four-vendor run was blocked before scoring any answer, so this result is not yet
+submission-ready.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 16/16 planted certificates verified; JSON round-trip also checked |
+| G2 | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and out-of-range corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | realistic fenced/prose response round-tripped |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 structured guesses; exact density `1/(2^72-1) = 2.1176e-22` |
+| G5 | demo has exactly 2 answers; shipping sample 0/200,000; reference cost 474,459 bit operations |
+| G6 | four attacks each 0/8; Gaussian reference 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 | doubled `d=144` instance built and verified; candidate-space bit length grew 73 to 145 |
+| G8 | 140/140 composed relabellings invariant and valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 24 characters, 2 atoms, about 6 tokens, 215 intended operations; all within caps |
+
+The four failing G6 attacks were: treating a generator outlier as `w`, a greedy
+pivot assignment without elimination, 256 random restarts, and the obvious
+all-ones/unit-vector/partial-XOR ansatz. Exact Gaussian elimination is deliberately
+outside `attacks`, because Track B expects it to succeed.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+The bare harness created genuine schema-v2 records, but OpenRouter rejected every
+request with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`. Errors do not consume an
+attempt and are not evidence of hardness.
+
+| bare call | preset | seed | model | scored | reason |
+|---:|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| 1 | easy | 1997605364 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | no | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| 2 | easy | 478069661 | OpenAI GPT-5.6 Terra | no | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| 3 | easy | 1681226860 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | no | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+| 4 | easy | 894850887 | OpenAI GPT-5.6 Terra | no | HTTP 403 key total limit |
+
+| G9 arm | solved / scored attempts | harness errors | verdict |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | 4 | not run |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | not run |
+| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | not run |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is therefore undefined, not zero. No conclusion about the
+claimed invariant intuition can be drawn until the key limit is replenished.
+The structural hint names only the global-XOR invariant; it does not give a
+multi-step procedure.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2007_08057 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=0, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+print(render(inst))
+candidate = parse_answer('<answer>["6",0]</answer>')
+assert verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, after a successful oracle rerun:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2007.08057 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is a succinct graph, not the paper’s usual explicit adjacency input. The
+  pendant reduction is central to Proposition 18, but the finite-field Cayley
+  source graph is generator-specific.
+- A tool makes the family easy: Gaussian elimination took milliseconds. This is
+  exactly why the declaration is Track B, never Track A.
+- The compact route still requires 215 exact 72-bit XORs at `easy`, close to the
+  300-operation cap. Failures may partly measure exact arithmetic stamina even
+  after the invariant is found.
+- The G4 prior is uniform over every stated nonzero functional and side. Its tiny
+  density does not model a solver biased toward XORs of visible masks.
+- SAT/ILP, Sherali-Adams, and the paper’s explicit-graph approximation were not
+  materialised on a `2^73`-vertex graph. The specialized exact linear solver is
+  the strongest applicable reference attack actually run.
+- `canonical_key` uses short GF(2)-dependence invariants. It is invariant under
+  tested affine relabellings but is not a complete Cayley-graph isomorphism test,
+  so unobserved collisions remain possible.
+- Most importantly, the oracle pool and all G9 arms are unscored because the
+  supplied OpenRouter key hit its total limit. Re-run all three arms before
+  trusting or submitting the hardness claim.
