-This module turns Aprile, Drescher, Fiorini, and Huynh’s [*A Tight Approximation
-Algorithm for the Cluster Vertex Deletion Problem*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.08057)
-into exact minimum-deletion problems. The solver receives a succinct Cayley graph
-on `GF(2)^d`, extended by one pendant vertex per original vertex, and returns a
-two-field symbolic description `[w,b]` of a parity-hyperplane deletion set. The
-checker uses only XOR, parity, and integer comparisons.
+## What this family asks
 
-The family is trustworthy by construction. Every generator mask `s` satisfies
-`<w,s>=1`, so every Cayley edge crosses the two parity classes and either class is
-a vertex cover. Any nonzero generator also pairs every `x` with `x XOR s`, giving
-a perfect matching of size `2^(d-1)` and proving that the cover is minimum.
-Proposition 18 proves that this cover is exactly a Cluster-VD hitting set after
-the pendant extension. The verifier executes the edge-alternation test and does
-not read `inst["answer"]`.
+The source is Aprile, Drescher, Fiorini, and Huynh, [*A Tight Approximation Algorithm for the Cluster Vertex Deletion Problem*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.08057). Section 1 defines a cluster graph as a disjoint union of cliques and a CVD hitting set as a vertex set whose deletion leaves a cluster graph, equivalently a set meeting every induced three-vertex path.
 
-## Why Track B
+An instance gives a succinct Cayley graph on `GF(2)^d`: its original vertices are all `d`-bit vectors, and each displayed nonzero mask `s` adds all edges `x--(x XOR s)`. One pendant vertex is then attached to every original vertex, exactly as in Proposition 18. The requested witness `[w,b]` denotes one parity hyperplane of original vertices. The checker accepts any nonzero `w` that has inner product one with every generator; either side `b` is valid.
 
-Section 1 defines Cluster-VD as minimum-cost deletion to a disjoint union of
-cliques. Theorem 1 gives a polynomial-time 2-approximation, Section 3 bounds its
-implementation by `O(N^4)`, and Section 1.3 records exact `1.811^k poly(N)` and
-`O(1.488^N)` algorithms. Those results make a Track A claim inappropriate for
-this specially structured distribution.
+Generation is inverse, not search: it samples `w` first, samples every generator from the affine hyperplane `<w,s>=1`, forces their total XOR to equal `w`, and requires full rank. Alternation makes the chosen parity side a vertex cover. Any one generator supplies a perfect matching of size `2^(d-1)`, proving the cover is minimum. Proposition 18 then makes it a minimum CVD hitting set. Verification is just exact hexadecimal decoding and GF(2) inner products; it never reads `inst["answer"]`.
 
-For the succinct instances here, the direct exact algorithm solves the `m` dense
-equations `<w,s>=1` by GF(2) Gaussian elimination in `O(m d^2)`. At provisional
-shipping parameters `d=72,m=216`, it solved 8/8 instances in 0.00818 seconds on
-average with 474,459 counted scalar-bit operations. The compressed route notices
-that the XOR of all masks is `w`, taking 215 word-XORs. This is within the no-tool
-cap but far beyond casual guessing; it is also the principal caveat of the family.
+## Why Track B, and what is easy
 
+This is not a Track A claim. The exact reference method two-colours the succinct Cayley graph by solving the `m` equations `<w,s>=1` with Gaussian elimination over `GF(2)`, taking `O(m d^2)` scalar-bit operations. At the shipping preset `d=72,m=216`, eight seeds averaged 474,459 counted scalar-bit operations (6,375 row XORs) and 0.0009 seconds. The compact route notices the planted global invariant and XORs the 216 displayed words, taking 215 word-XOR operations. The gap—dense elimination versus one accumulator—is the claimed no-tool compression.
+
+The paper itself gives several warnings against an overbroad hardness claim. Theorem 1 and Section 3 give an `O(N^4)` 2-approximation on an explicit `N`-vertex graph. Section 1.3 records exact `1.811^k N^O(1)` and `O(1.488^N)` algorithms. Lemma 12 solves paths and cycles exactly in polynomial time, while the conclusion leaves chordal and chordal-`2P3`-free exact complexity open. The paper's UGC and LP lower bounds are worst-case results; they do not prove this generated distribution hard.
